      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 33,
      "san": "Rg1",
      "human_san": "Rg1",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "Flohr: \"Up to now I was satisfied with\nmy play. But castling now would be stronger because, after moving the Rook to\nf3, there would be more opportunities for an attack. I played this move\nbecause I expected some defensive reply from my opponent. And he did not\ndisappoint me.\"",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "",
      "system_symbol": null,
      "is_key_moment": false,
      "final_comment": false,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": true,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 34,
      "san": "Bf8",
      "human_san": "Bf8",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "Clearly not expecting White's reply. 17...Kh8 was\nneeded and Flohr should have protected the f4 Pawn with e2-e3 (not necessary\nif he had castled at the 17th move).",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Black missed the potential to win a piece for a pawn and target the bishop on e4 with 17...Kh8, which leads to a slight advantage for Black [pv:Kh8 Nxg7 Nxg7 Be4 Bd6]; instead, 17...Bf8 lets the advantage slip away to equality (-0.40 \u2192 +0.20, Stockfish:16) along [pv:Bf8 Bc6 Nd4 Bxd4 Bxf5]. The retreat immediately improves Black's piece activity, but in the line Black's pieces may grow passive and Black may lose the bishop-pair edge, while White's pieces could also turn passive as White's two-bishop advantage fades. Kh8 leads to a slight advantage for Black [pv:Kh8 Nxg7 Nxg7 Be4 Bd6]\u2014the stronger continuation Black passed up.",
      "system_symbol": "?!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": true,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false,
      "text_ratio": 0.0377,
      "text_jaccard": 0.0597
    },
