      "system_only": false,
      "neither": true
    },
    {
      "ply": 49,
      "san": "Nxd8",
      "human_san": "Nxd8",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "White's Nxd8 holds the balance (+0.24, Stockfish:16), leaving Black with doubled c-pawns\u2014often awkward to advance and defend\u2014while [pv:Nxd8 bxc6 Kxe2 Rxb2 Rb1] may set White's sights on the rook at b2; in return, Black immediately gets a rook on the seventh rank at b2. Nh6+, which leads to equality via [pv:Nh6+ Kh8 Nf7+ Kg8 Nxd8], was a comparable alternative, yet Nxd8 does more right away by inflicting the doubled c-pawns. The queens are off \u2014 the game enters its final phase, where technique matters more than tactics. The position stays balanced.",
      "system_symbol": null,
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 50,
      "san": "Nxg1",
      "human_san": "Nxg1",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "Perhaps 25... bxc6 was better but after 26.Kxe2 Ba6+ 27. Ke3 Rxd8\n28. d4 the White Rook is in action also.",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Black wins a pawn with 25...Nxg1, but the move concedes a clear advantage to White (+0.24 \u2192 +1.47, Stockfish:16); [pv:Nxg1 Bd5+ Kh8 Nf7+ Kg8] runs into Bd5+, which punishes the capture as Black's king could come under heavy pressure deeper in the line. Black missed the chance to play bxc6, which leads to equality in [pv:bxc6 Kxe2 Rxb2 a3 Bd6], stripping White of the bishop-pair edge and opening the b-file for the rook.",
      "system_symbol": "?",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": true,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false,
      "text_ratio": 0.0379,
      "text_jaccard": 0.0588
    },
    {
      "ply": 51,
      "san": "Bd5+",
      "human_san": "Bd5+",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Bd5+ is the most accurate move, maintaining White's advantage (+1.51, Stockfish:16) in [pv:Bd5+ Kh8 Nf7+ Kg8 Kf2], at the cost of White's king potentially coming under pressure further along that line. Be5 leads to equality [pv:Be5 fxe5 Bd5+ Kh8 Nf7+]; although it immediately leaves Black with a weaker pawn structure\u2014doubled, isolated, and backward pawns, the kind of long-term damage that rarely outweighs giving up a lasting edge\u2014that line is clearly weaker than the accurate check.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
