    {
      "ply": 56,
      "san": "Nh3+",
      "human_san": "Nh3+",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "The check 28...Nh3+ forces White's king to g3, but the move still leads to a clear advantage for White (+1.29, Stockfish:16); the continuation [pv:Nh3+ Kg3 Be7 Nd6+ Kf8] may see White establish the knight on d6 with check while Black's king retreats to f8. It was nonetheless Black's best try\u2014Ne2 gives White a winning advantage [pv:Ne2 Kxe2 Be7 Nd6+ Kf8]\u2014since the forcing check avoids the immediate knight exchange on e2 that would leave White even further ahead.",
      "system_symbol": null,
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 57,
      "san": "Kg3",
      "human_san": "Kg3",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "so Knight's fate is decided already.",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "The most accurate move is Kg3, which maintains White's advantage (+1.26, Stockfish:16) in [pv:Kg3 Nxf4 Kxf4 Be7 Nd6+], immediately targeting the knight on h3 and keeping that piece under pressure\u2014a target that often ties Black down for several moves. In return, the line may leave Black with a passed pawn on f6. Kf3 leads only to a slight advantage for White in [pv:Kf3 h5 Nd6+ Kh8 Nxc8], where White might later target the rook on c8 and could reduce Black's bishop-pair edge, but that plan is slower and less forcing than the knight pressure Kg3 applies right away.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": true,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false,
      "text_ratio": 0.0166,
      "text_jaccard": 0.0
    },
    {
      "ply": 58,
      "san": "b5",
      "human_san": "b5",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "29...b5 makes matters worse, and White now has a decisive advantage ((+1.26 \u2192 +3.01, Stockfish:16); [pv:b5 Nd6+ Kh8 Re1 Nxf4]). Nd6+ is the refutation, and in the line White may pin a black piece. Passed pawns can become long-term assets, so Black missed the chance to create one on f6 with 29...Nxf4, which leads to a clear advantage for White ([pv:Nxf4 Kxf4 Be7 Nd6+ Kf8]), and missed the potential to leave a White piece hanging deeper in that variation\u2014while later in the game the position can still devolve into forcing checks that keep Black's king under pressure.",
      "system_symbol": "?",
      "is_key_moment": true,
